  "prediction": "The report provides an overview of the methods and data sources used to measure the size of the federal workforce in the United States. The report compares the data from the Office of Management and Budget (OMB) and the Office of Personnel Management (OPM), focusing on the differences in their methodologies and the resulting data.\n\nThe report explains that OMB uses the Full-Time Equivalent (FTE) method to measure the size of the federal workforce, while OPM uses the on-board employment method. The FTE method calculates the number of full-time employees by adding the hours worked by part-time employees and dividing by the number of hours in a full-time workweek. The on-board employment method, on the other hand, counts the number of employees who are currently employed by the federal government.\n\nThe report also highlights the differences in the data availability and accessibility between OMB and OPM. OPM's Federal Employee Data System (FEDS) is the most comprehensive source of federal employment data, providing detailed information on the size, demographics, and compensation of the federal workforce. OMB's data, on the other hand, is primarily available in the President's Budget and is focused on the FTE count of the federal workforce.\n\nThe report concludes by emphasizing the importance of understanding the differences in methodologies and data sources when using federal workforce statistics. Users should be aware that the data from OMB and OPM may not be directly comparable and should consult the appropriate source depending on their specific research question.",
  "scored_prediction": "The report provides an overview of the methods and data sources used to measure the size of the federal workforce in the United States. The report compares the data from the Office of Management and Budget (OMB) and the Office of Personnel Management (OPM), focusing on the differences in their methodologies and the resulting data.\n\nThe report explains that OMB uses the Full-Time Equivalent (FTE) method to measure the size of the federal workforce, while OPM uses the on-board employment method. The FTE method calculates the number of full-time employees by adding the hours worked by part-time employees and dividing by the number of hours in a full-time workweek. The on-board employment method, on the other hand, counts the number of employees who are currently employed by the federal government.\n\nThe report also highlights the differences in the data availability and accessibility between OMB and OPM. OPM's Federal Employee Data System (FEDS) is the most comprehensive source of federal employment data, providing detailed information on the size, demographics, and compensation of the federal workforce. OMB's data, on the other hand, is primarily available in the President's Budget and is focused on the FTE count of the federal workforce.\n\nThe report concludes by emphasizing the importance of understanding the differences in methodologies and data sources when using federal workforce statistics. Users should be aware that the data from OMB and OPM may not be directly comparable and should consult the appropriate source depending on their specific research question.",
  "score": 27.1739125834121,
  "answers": [
    "This report describes online tools, reports, and data compilations created by the Office of Management and Budget (OMB) and the Office of Personnel Management (OPM) that contain statistics about federal employees and the federal workforce. The report also describes key characteristics of each resource and briefly discusses selected methodological differences, with the intention of facilitating the selection of appropriate data for specific purposes. This report is not intended to be a definitive list of all information on the federal workforce. It describes significant and recurring products that contain specific data often requested by Members or congressional staff."
  ],
